// Generated by scripts/research/build_ycjdt_site.py. Do not edit by hand.
export const ARTICLE_HTML = "<nav class=\"sitenav\"><a href=\"/\">Community Archive</a> <span>/</span> <a href=\"/research\">Research</a></nav>\n<div class=\"wrap\">\n<header>\n  <span class=\"eyebrow\">Community Archive Research · November 2026</span>\n  <h1 data-edit=\"e1\">You can just do things</h1>\n  <div class=\"hgrid\">\n    <div class=\"hmain\">\n      <p class=\"lede\" data-edit=\"e2\">“You can just do things” is about agency: a reminder that you have it, which is not most people's default belief. It showed up during lockdown in the part of Twitter known as TPOT.</p>\n      <p class=\"lede\" data-edit=\"e62\">TPOT is the part of Twitter between rationalist blogs, meditation, therapy, AI research. We think it is unusually generative as a scene, and this phrase is one example. In five years it went from encouraging generic agency among online nerds, to a tech startup mantra, to being tweeted by the president of El Salvador and the CTO of the Department of War.</p>\n      <p class=\"lede\" data-edit=\"e60\">My friend Guy asked me to map how this meme spread and changed. This is that map, drawn from <span data-n=\"union\"></span> tweets. It is also a demonstration of what the Community Archive is for: tracing where ideas come from.</p>\n      <h2 class=\"mini\" data-edit=\"e63\">Takeaways</h2>\n      <ul class=\"takeaways\">\n        <li data-edit=\"e3\"><b>It started in TPOT.</b> Almost nobody said it before 2020, and through 2023, Community Archive members wrote <span data-n=\"memshare2023\"></span> of all uses.</li>\n        <li data-edit=\"e4\"><b>Then it spread outward: first to tech, then politics.</b> The closer an account sits to archive members, the earlier it picked the phrase up. Use took off in October 2024 and peaked in February 2025.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"hside\">\n      <div id=\"inciting\"></div>\n      <div class=\"figkey\" id=\"figkey\"></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <details class=\"terms\" id=\"terms\">\n    <summary data-edit=\"e6\">Terms</summary>\n    <dl>\n      <dt data-edit=\"e8\">TPOT</dt><dd data-edit=\"e7\">“This part of Twitter”: a loose scene of a few thousand accounts around rationalist blogs, meditation, therapy talk and startups.</dd>\n      <dt data-edit=\"e10\">Community Archive</dt><dd data-edit=\"e9\">An open database of 27M tweets from 2.4k members that we're using as a proxy for TPOT.</dd>\n      <dt data-edit=\"e12\">Greater TPOT</dt><dd data-edit=\"e11\">The 153k accounts that have a mutual interaction with a Community Archive member, plus the members. We have 230 million of their tweets, to Aug 24, 2026. Also called the one-hop database.</dd>\n      <dt data-edit=\"e14\">The map</dt><dd data-edit=\"e13\">The archive's map of Twitter. Each hexagon is a cluster of accounts that talk to each other. Accounts in adjacent hexagons are more likely to talk to each other. We divide them into 38 regions that we colour as four families.</dd>\n    </dl>\n  </details>\n</header>\n\n<div class=\"slot\" data-edit=\"s1\" data-slot></div>\n\n<figure class=\"fig wide\" aria-labelledby=\"fig1-title\">\n  <div class=\"fig-head\">\n    <h2 id=\"fig1-title\" data-edit=\"e15\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">Figure 1</span> How “You can just do things” spread from TPOT to the mainstream</h2>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"panels\" id=\"panels\"></div>\n  <div class=\"legend figlegend\" id=\"famlegend\"></div>\n  <figcaption class=\"small muted\" data-edit=\"e18\">Each hexagon is a cluster of accounts on the archive's map; it is lit if someone in it used the phrase in that period and faded if someone had earlier. The bar splits each period's uses by family.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<div class=\"slot\" data-edit=\"s2\" data-slot></div>\n\n<figure class=\"fig spaced\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t\">\n  <div class=\"fig-head\">\n    <h2 id=\"f1t\" class=\"h3ish\" data-edit=\"e22\">Uses per month, 2020 to August 2026</h2>\n  </div>\n  <svg id=\"chart\" class=\"strip\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Monthly uses of the phrase, 2020 to August 2026, members and everyone else\"></svg>\n  <figcaption class=\"small muted\" data-edit=\"e23\"><span class=\"legend\" style=\"display:inline-flex;margin-right:10px\"><span><span class=\"sw\" style=\"background:var(--tpot)\"></span>Archive members</span><span><span class=\"sw\" style=\"background:var(--other)\"></span>Everyone else</span></span><span data-n=\"hopn\"></span> tweets from <span data-n=\"hopaccts\"></span> accounts in greater TPOT (archive members plus every account that has had a mutual interaction with one): about 3 a month in 2022, 170 in 2024, 620 in 2025. The database holds twice as many tweets for 2026 as for 2025, so per tweet the phrase peaked in February 2025 and has since fallen by three-quarters.</figcaption>\n  <details class=\"how animtoggle\"><summary data-edit=\"e61\">Play the spread on the map, month by month</summary>\n  <div class=\"anim\">\n    <div class=\"animmap\"><svg id=\"anim\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Animated map of where the phrase had been used, month by month\"></svg></div>\n    <div class=\"animctl\">\n      <h3 data-edit=\"e16\">Watch it spread</h3>\n      <span class=\"animmonth\" id=\"animmonth\"></span>\n      <span class=\"small muted\" id=\"animcount\"></span>\n      <div class=\"row\"><button id=\"animplay\" class=\"nav\" type=\"button\">Play</button><input id=\"animrange\" type=\"range\" min=\"0\" max=\"79\" value=\"79\" aria-label=\"Month\"></div>\n      <p class=\"small muted\" data-edit=\"e17\">A hexagon lights in the month its cluster first uses the phrase and stays lit.</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  </details>\n  <details class=\"how\"><summary data-edit=\"e24\">How we counted</summary>\n    <p data-edit=\"e25\">A tweet counts if it contains “can just do things” or a close variant: “stuff” or “shit” for “things”; an adverb before or after “just” (“can literally just do things”, “can, in fact, just do things”); punctuation or emphasis inside the sentence (“can just… do things”, “can. just. do. things”, “can just *do* things”); and “can just not do things”. Negations (“can't just do things”) are left out. That is 170 distinct surface forms. Retweets are excluded; replies and quote tweets are included.</p>\n    <p data-edit=\"e26\">We read 210 randomly drawn matches from 2023 on (60 from the whole period and 150 from 2025–26): 205 were clearly the slogan, 2 used the words in their ordinary sense (“an agent that can just do things on my behalf”) and 3 were borderline. So roughly 2% of the count is noise, and it could be as much as 5%. Before 2023 there are only 67 matches, so we read every one and removed 11 that used the words in their ordinary sense (“a computer can just do things quicker”).</p></details>\n</figure>\n\n<div class=\"slot\" data-edit=\"s101\" data-slot></div>\n\n<section>\n  <span class=\"eyebrow\">Timeline</span>\n  <h2 data-edit=\"e27\">Seven stages, in the posters' own words</h2>\n  <p data-edit=\"e28\">Two tweets are shown for each stage; the rest open on request.</p>\n  <div id=\"timeline\"></div>\n</section>\n\n<figure class=\"fig\" aria-labelledby=\"f3t\">\n  <div class=\"fig-head\">\n    <span class=\"eyebrow\">Figure 2</span>\n    <h2 id=\"f3t\" data-edit=\"e29\">The takeoff had no single trigger</h2>\n    <p data-edit=\"e30\">Uses per day, September 2024 to January 2025. Daily use roughly doubled in mid-October, held through the election, doubled again in the week Luigi Mangione was arrested, and was already at that level when Sam Altman posted the bare phrase.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"card\"><svg id=\"daily\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Daily uses, September 2024 to January 2025, with events marked\"></svg></div>\n  <figcaption class=\"small muted\" data-edit=\"e31\">Average uses per day: 3.4 in September, 10.7 from Oct 13 to 31, 12.1 in November, 29.3 from Dec 9 to 20, 36.6 from Dec 21 to 31. The event labels are our reading of what people were posting about; the chart shows timing, not cause.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<figure class=\"fig\" aria-labelledby=\"f4t\">\n  <div class=\"fig-head\">\n    <span class=\"eyebrow\">Figure 3 · inside the archive versus outside</span>\n    <h2 id=\"f4t\" data-edit=\"e32\">The closer to the archive's members, the earlier and the more</h2>\n    <p data-edit=\"e33\">Uses per million tweets, by quarter, for four rings of accounts. In 2023 members used the phrase hundreds of times per million tweets while accounts tied to only a few members almost never did. Each ring out rises later. By 2026 the rings are within about three times of each other.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"card\"><svg id=\"bands\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Uses per million tweets by quarter for members and three rings of neighbours, on a logarithmic scale\"></svg>\n  <p class=\"small muted\" data-edit=\"e34\">The vertical scale is logarithmic: each gridline is ten times the one below. Quarters with fewer than five uses in a ring are drawn hollow.</p></div>\n  <div class=\"card\"><h3 data-edit=\"e35\">When each ring picked it up</h3><div id=\"ever\"></div></div>\n  <details class=\"how\"><summary data-edit=\"e36\">What this can and can't show</summary>\n    <p data-edit=\"e37\">This compares accounts inside one database, so the rings are counted the same way. It replaces an earlier comparison against random archive accounts, which did not hold up once accounts were matched on how many of their tweets the archive holds.</p>\n    <p data-edit=\"e38\">Two limits. The ties are measured over 2022 to 2026 as a whole, so an account can be “close” partly because of exchanges that came after it used the phrase. And every account here is within one exchange of a member; nobody truly far from TPOT is in the data. The gradient is consistent with the phrase spreading through conversation with members. It is also what you would see if accounts similar to members simply share their tastes.</p></details>\n</figure>\n\n<div class=\"slot\" data-edit=\"s102\" data-slot></div>\n\n<section>\n  <span class=\"eyebrow\">Wording</span>\n  <h2 data-edit=\"e39\">Two variants, “things” and “stuff”, and one won</h2>\n  <p data-edit=\"e40\">Inside TPOT the phrase came in two wordings from the start. In August 2022 Nat Sharpe posted a video captioned “you can just do stuff”, eight members retweeted it, and TPOT began calling it “Nat's Law”. Over the following year “stuff” was the slightly more common form, and ten weeks after the video Sam Altman used it too. When use took off in late 2024 it was “things” that spread, and “stuff” fell to one use in twenty.</p>\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3 data-edit=\"e41\">Share of uses saying “stuff” rather than “things”, by year</h3>\n    <div class=\"legend\"><span><span class=\"sw\" style=\"background:var(--oth)\"></span>“do stuff”</span><span><span class=\"sw\" style=\"background:var(--tpot)\"></span>“do things”</span></div>\n    <div class=\"mini\" id=\"stuffmini\"></div>\n    <p class=\"small muted\" data-edit=\"e42\">Before the video (Aug 29, 2022): 13 of 41 uses said “stuff”. In the year after: 52 of 96. In 2025: 5%. Counting only the exact sentence “you can just do things” or “you can just do stuff” gives the same picture: 55% “stuff” in 2022, 39% in 2023, 14% in 2024 and 4% in 2025.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"two\">\n    <div class=\"lineage\"><h3 data-edit=\"e43\"><span class=\"sw\" style=\"background:var(--tpot)\"></span>“do things”</h3><div class=\"stack\" id=\"lin-things\"></div></div>\n    <div class=\"lineage\"><h3 data-edit=\"e44\"><span class=\"sw\" style=\"background:var(--oth)\"></span>“do stuff”</h3><div class=\"stack\" id=\"lin-stuff\"></div></div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"small muted\" data-edit=\"e45\">Nat's original tweet is in neither database; its date, wording and video are known from the eight retweets. The video looks like a repost of a vertical clip. One 2024 reply traces it to the comedian Rodney D. Norman, which we could not confirm.</p>\n</section>\n\n<div class=\"slot\" data-edit=\"s103\" data-slot></div>\n\n<section>\n  <span class=\"eyebrow\">Before TPOT</span>\n  <h2 data-edit=\"e46\">Amanda Palmer, 2015</h2>\n  <p data-edit=\"e47\">The earliest use in the archive is a 2015 tweet that quotes Amanda Palmer's tumblr.</p>\n  <div class=\"stack\" id=\"lin-pre\" style=\"max-width:560px\"></div>\n  <blockquote class=\"pull\" data-edit=\"e48\">…that thinking that is what makes life bearable, when you realize you can just Do Things.<cite>Amanda Palmer, Oct 28, 2015 · <a href=\"https://amandapalmer.tumblr.com/post/132059138441\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tumblr</a></cite></blockquote>\n  <p data-edit=\"e49\">It has the same register as TPOT's first uses: permission, not ambition. We found no recorded path from it into TPOT's 2020 uses, and the sentence is ordinary enough English to have been coined more than once. What argues against many independent coinages is how rare it was: our greater-TPOT data holds between one and five million tweets a year for 2017 to 2019 and not one slogan use.</p>\n</section>\n\n<div class=\"slot\" data-edit=\"s104\" data-slot></div>\n\n<section>\n  <span class=\"eyebrow\">How TPOT remembers it</span>\n  <h2 data-edit=\"e50\">Advice that outgrew its first audience</h2>\n  <div class=\"two\" id=\"lin-later\"></div>\n</section>\n\n<div class=\"slot\" data-edit=\"s105\" data-slot></div>\n\n<section>\n  <span class=\"eyebrow\">Search the archive</span>\n  <h2>Find the tweets yourself</h2>\n  <p>This runs the Community Archive's own search, live, so it reflects what the archive holds today. Searching needs a sign-in.</p>\n  <form class=\"controls\" id=\"sform\">\n    <input id=\"q\" type=\"search\" value=\"can just do things\" aria-label=\"Search the archive\">\n    <select id=\"sort\" aria-label=\"Sort\"><option value=\"likes\">Most liked</option><option value=\"oldest\">Oldest first</option><option value=\"newest\">Newest first</option></select>\n    <button id=\"sgo\" class=\"nav\" type=\"submit\">Search</button>\n  </form>\n  <span id=\"count\" class=\"small muted\"></span>\n  <div id=\"list\" class=\"stack\"></div>\n  <button id=\"more\" hidden>Show 20 more</button>\n</section>\n\n<div class=\"slot\" data-edit=\"s106\" data-slot></div>\n\n<section>\n  <span class=\"eyebrow\">Method and limits</span>\n  <h2 data-edit=\"e53\">What this can and can't show</h2>\n  <ul class=\"caveats\">\n    <li data-edit=\"e54\"><b>Two sources.</b> The counts and Figures 1 to 3 come from the greater-TPOT data (to Aug 24, 2026). The quotes after that date and the 2015 and 2017 tweets come from the Community Archive (to Nov 10, 2026). Together they hold <span data-n=\"union\"></span> distinct uses.</li>\n    <li data-edit=\"e55\"><b>Everything here is near TPOT.</b> Both sources are built outward from archive members. “First” means first in these sources, not first on Twitter, and uses by people far from this scene are missing at every date.</li>\n    <li data-edit=\"e56\"><b>Absence of a link is not evidence of none.</b> Language spreads by reading. We only see replies, mentions and quotes.</li>\n    <li data-edit=\"e57\"><b>The fall after early 2025 is a fall in rate.</b> Raw counts stay high because the database holds twice as many tweets for 2026 as for 2025. The rate falls inside every ring, including members, so it is not only an effect of who was added.</li>\n    <li data-edit=\"e58\"><b>Likes are as last observed</b>, not final, and follower counts are as of each tweet.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p class=\"small muted\" data-edit=\"e59\"><b>How this was made.</b> Research and drafting by AI agents (Claude) directed and checked by the archive's maintainer. Every number on the page is produced by a script from a frozen copy of the data; the matcher and the scripts are kept with the piece. <b>Changes since draft 1:</b> the tweet set was rebuilt from a larger database with a wider match (3,466 to <span data-n=\"union\"></span> uses); the keyword-based “political” colour was dropped; a comparison against random accounts was withdrawn; the takeoff was re-dated from December to October 2024. Corrections are welcome.</p>\n</section>\n</div>\n<div id=\"tip\" hidden></div>"
